(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["interpretation-of-medical-literature-exam-1-prevention-screening-quiz-version-2"]={"title":"Prevention and Screening Quiz 2 — Interpretation of Medical Literature","category":"Interpretation of Medical Literature","exam":"Exam 1","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"A clinician advises a healthy smoker on quitting and on diet. Which type of clinical prevention is this?","choices":["Behavioral or lifestyle counseling","Immunization","Screening for early disease","Chemoprevention with a medication"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Advice aimed at changing habits is its own type of prevention, and counseling approaches are studied with randomized controlled trials. — 8 PAJ5512 Prevention MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 4"},{"q":"A town screens all adults for the first time and finds 40 cases. Two years later it repeats the screen and finds 12 cases among people previously negative. Which measure do the 12 cases reflect?","choices":["Prevalence","The false-positive rate","The case fatality rate","Incidence"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. They are new cases arising over a two-year period, which is what a repeat screening detects. — 8 PAJ5512 Prevention MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"Why should a screening test have high specificity?","choices":["To reduce false negatives","To reduce false positives","To detect disease earlier","To make the test cheaper"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. In a screened population most people are healthy, so specificity is what keeps healthy people from being wrongly flagged. — 8 PAJ5512 Prevention MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"A patient with diagnosed diabetes has regular eye and foot examinations to prevent complications. Which level of prevention is this?","choices":["Primary","Secondary","Screening","Tertiary"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, because clinical intervention after diagnosis to reduce complications is the definition of tertiary prevention. — 8 PAJ5512 Prevention MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 5"},{"q":"What is the objective of a community screening program?","choices":["Diagnose symptomatic patients in detail","Treat advanced disease in hospital","Test many people at little or no cost","Screen a few selected patients at high cost"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. It takes place in a community setting and aims to reach large numbers of participants cheaply. — 8 PAJ5512 Prevention MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"Which three areas decide whether a condition belongs in preventive care?","choices":["Disease rarity, test novelty, specialist supply","Burden of suffering, test quality, treatment","Insurance cover, patient demand, test speed","Test price, patient age, personal preference"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The burden it causes, the quality of any screening test, and the effectiveness, safety and cost of treatment are the three criteria. — 8 PAJ5512 Prevention MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 7"},{"q":"What follows from a false-positive screening result?","choices":["Expense and unnecessary interventions","Delayed diagnosis of real disease","Falsely reassured patients","Longer survival after diagnosis"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. A healthy person flagged by mistake is subjected to further testing and possibly treatment they did not need. — 8 PAJ5512 Prevention MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"A healthy patient at raised risk starts a daily medication solely to lower the chance of future disease. Which type of clinical prevention is this?","choices":["Immunization","Chemoprevention","Screening","Behavioral or lifestyle counseling"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Giving a drug to healthy people to reduce their chance of developing disease is the chemoprevention type. — 8 PAJ5512 Prevention MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 4"},{"q":"Drug A has a number needed to harm of 500 for a side effect and Drug B has 50. Which conclusion follows?","choices":["Drug B causes the side effect less often","The two drugs are equally safe","Drug A is more effective","Drug A causes the side effect less often"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. One patient in 500 is harmed on Drug A compared with one in 50 on Drug B, so a higher number needed to harm is safer. — 8 PAJ5512 Prevention MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"What must be true about screening benefits and harms?","choices":["Costs may be ignored","Benefits must outweigh costs and harms","Harms are acceptable if benefits are possible","Benefit need only be shown in the laboratory"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Screening exposes many healthy people to risk, so the balance has to favor benefit. — 8 PAJ5512 Prevention MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 29"},{"q":"What does the first screening of a population detect?","choices":["Incident cases","Only false-positive cases","Prevalent cases","Only fast-growing disease"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The first screening finds the disease already present in the population, and prevalence counts everyone affected at one point in time. — 8 PAJ5512 Prevention MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"What must be clear before a screening program starts?","choices":["Who follows up the results","Who paid for the equipment","Who will publish the results","Who will attend most often"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. It should also be clear what counts as a positive test and how findings become part of the participant's medical record. — 8 PAJ5512 Prevention MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"What must be true for people who have a positive screening result?","choices":["They must pay for confirmation themselves","They must have access to care","They must be excluded from the program","They must repeat the same screen yearly"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. A positive screen is pointless if nothing can follow it, so access to care is required. — 8 PAJ5512 Prevention MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"For a screening test to have value, what must be shown about outcomes?","choices":["They are the same however early disease is found","Survival after diagnosis is longer","Every case of disease is found","They are better when disease is found early"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Outcomes must be better when disease is identified and treated early than when it is found later. — 8 PAJ5512 Prevention MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 10"},{"q":"Why do rapidly developing conditions often escape screening?","choices":["They produce no detectable change","They cause symptoms between screens","They are always benign","They are found only after death"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Rapid development usually produces symptoms between screens, so the condition is diagnosed clinically instead. — 8 PAJ5512 Prevention MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 20"},{"q":"The number needed to harm for a side effect is 200. If 1,000 patients receive the intervention, how many are expected to be harmed?","choices":["5 patients","200,000 patients","0.2 patients","200 patients"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The number treated divided by the number needed to harm is 1,000 divided by 200, which is 5 patients harmed. — 8 PAJ5512 Prevention MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"If early treatment after screening is not effective, how does screening affect the time of death?","choices":["It does not change it","It brings death forward","It delays death","It makes death impossible to date"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Diagnosis comes earlier but death occurs at the same time, so survival after diagnosis looks longer without anyone living longer. — 8 PAJ5512 Prevention MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"Which is a potential advantage of screening?","choices":["Disease can be treated earlier","Every abnormal result reflects real disease","Lead-time bias is removed","No follow-up testing is needed"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Finding disease while it is asymptomatic gives an opportunity for treatment early in its course, which pays off when early treatment works better. — 8 PAJ5512 Prevention MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 10"},{"q":"Which study design typically evaluates immunizations?","choices":["Case series","Cross-sectional survey","Randomized controlled trial","Case-control study"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Immunization studies are typically randomized controlled trials, as are counseling approaches. — 8 PAJ5512 Prevention MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"Why might a cohort design be chosen to study a screening program?","choices":["It assigns participants to screening at random","Effectiveness can take years to determine","It needs no follow-up period","It cannot be affected by bias"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. A cohort follows people forward over time, which suits a question whose answer takes years to appear. — 8 PAJ5512 Prevention MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 10"},{"q":"Why does lead-time bias make screening appear to lengthen survival?","choices":["Screened patients live longer","Slow tumors are found more often","Screened patients take better care of themselves","Patients know their diagnosis for longer"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The apparent gain in survival is due to the longer time spent knowing the diagnosis, and not to living longer. — 8 PAJ5512 Prevention MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"Which conditions does screening tend to detect, giving rise to length-time bias?","choices":["Rapid conditions with poor prognoses","Conditions with no prognostic difference","Conditions that are never diagnosed","Slow, favorable-prognosis conditions"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Slow conditions are present for longer, so a screen is likely to catch them, and they carry better prognoses than fast ones. — 8 PAJ5512 Prevention MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"Which is a component of the burden of suffering from a condition?","choices":["Test simplicity","Test sensitivity","Disability","Treatment cost-effectiveness"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Death, disease, disability, discomfort, dissatisfaction and destitution are the components of burden, together with measures such as hospitalizations and incidence. — 8 PAJ5512 Prevention MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 8"},{"q":"The number needed to treat for a preventive drug is 20. If 400 patients take it for the same period, how many cases are expected to be prevented?","choices":["20 cases","8,000 cases","80 cases","200 cases"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The number treated divided by the number needed to treat is 400 divided by 20, which is 20 cases prevented. — 8 PAJ5512 Prevention MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"A clinic gives an unvaccinated adult a hepatitis B vaccine series. Which type of clinical prevention is this?","choices":["Screening","Chemoprevention","Behavioral counseling","Immunization"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Vaccination is the immunization type of prevention, and its effect is usually established in randomized controlled trials. — 8 PAJ5512 Prevention MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 4"},{"q":"How can a program reduce the problem of false positives from low prevalence?","choices":["Screen only people without risk factors","Screen higher-risk populations","Use a test with lower specificity","Stop calculating predictive values"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Prevalence is higher in higher-risk groups, so a larger share of positive results are true. — 8 PAJ5512 Prevention MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"Which is a component of the quality of a screening test?","choices":["Mortality rate","Hospitalizations","Acceptability","Effectiveness of treatment"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Sensitivity, specificity, simplicity, cost, safety and acceptability are the six components of screening test quality. — 8 PAJ5512 Prevention MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 8"},{"q":"For secondary prevention, which extra requirement applies to treatment after a condition is found?","choices":["Treatment is given only in hospital","Treatment is offered only after symptoms begin","Early treatment works better than later treatment","Treatment carries no cost to patients"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Treatment after screening must be more effective than treatment given later without screening, once the patient becomes symptomatic. — 8 PAJ5512 Prevention MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 8"},{"q":"What happens to positive predictive value when the same test is used in a higher-risk population?","choices":["It falls","It is unchanged","It rises","It becomes equal to sensitivity"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Higher prevalence means a larger share of positive results are true positives, while sensitivity and specificity stay the same. — 8 PAJ5512 Prevention MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"A healthy adult receives an influenza vaccine. Which level of prevention is this?","choices":["Primary","Secondary","Tertiary","Chemoprevention"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, because vaccination removes the risk of infection before any disease exists, which is the aim of primary prevention. — 8 PAJ5512 Prevention MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 5"}]};
